{
	"info": {
		"_postman_id": "buildiq-rbac-collection",
		"name": "Buildiq RBAC — REQ-OBRBAC-002..007",
		"description": "Newman integration suite for the buildiq-rbac OpenSpec change. Exercises the manifest-endpoint security boundary (403 before 404 disambiguation, viewer/editor/owner read paths, audited admin bypass, transfer-ownership, list-endpoint IDOR closure) over real HTTP against a live Nextcloud instance with the `buildiq` app enabled. The collection is self-contained: a Setup folder provisions a fresh seed Application, two test users, and a viewer/editor group; a Teardown folder removes every artefact so the suite is idempotent. Reuses the `admin/admin` credentials from the dev compose (memory rule).\n\n⚠️ A PER-REQUEST BASIC AUTH OVERRIDE DOES NOT CHANGE WHO THE REQUEST IS.\n\nNewman keeps ONE cookie jar per `newman run`, keyed by origin. Nextcloud only consults the `Authorization:` header when there is no session — `OC::handleRequest()` calls `handleLogin()` (and through it `tryBasicAuthLogin()`) exclusively in the `else` branch of `if (Server::get(IUserSession::class)->isLoggedIn())`, server/lib/base.php:1054-1066. The Setup folder authenticates as admin, so by the time a later request carries `{{outsider_user}}` credentials for the SAME origin, Newman is already holding an admin session cookie and the request executes AS ADMIN. The override is silently ignored.\n\nThat is not a hypothetical: it is why `2.3` and `3.1` were red in CI while `2.1`, `2.2` and `3.2` were green. Admin sees every app (list bypass, ApplicationsController::listMine) and clears getManifest via the audited admin bypass (ApplicationsController:1268-1271) — so the outsider assertions saw admin's answers, and the viewer/editor assertions PASSED VACUOUSLY, measuring admin rather than the role they name.\n\nThe fix is the same one buildiq-application-versions.postman_collection.json documents and measures for the anonymous case: reach the SAME server by its OTHER name. `{{outsiderBase}}` is `http://127.0.0.1:8080`, a distinct origin whose cookie jar is empty, so the outsider's Basic credentials are the only thing there and Nextcloud authenticates them fresh. Request `2.2b` is the positive control — it asserts the acting user really is `rbac-outsider` before any conclusion is drawn from `2.3`/`3.1`. Without it, this collection could go back to measuring admin and report green.\n\n📋 KNOWN LIMITATION, STATED RATHER THAN HIDDEN. Only two origins are usable here (`localhost` and `127.0.0.1`; both are trusted domains, a third alias is not). The outsider needs one of them, so `2.1`, `2.2`, `3.2` and `4.2` still run as admin and remain vacuous — they pass, but they are NOT evidence about the viewer or editor role. Tracked as work, not as coverage.",
		"schema": "https://schema.getpostman.com/json/collection/v2.1.0/collection.json"
	},
